helper: poll less often while the panel is off ([poller] poll_s / poll_s_display_off)

Out-of-tree patch for the y700 (TB323FU) running tb323fu-linux helper 0.4.1
(upstream main 0c7c0c4, 2026-10-13). Not submitted upstream; the owner accepts
out-of-tree patches. See y700/linux/power/WAKEUPS.md section 2.2.

Why: with the screen off the tablet idles for hours without suspending
(README "永不休眠"). tb323fu-helperd then still wakes a CPU every 5 s from two
threads - the poll loop in main.rs (profile/thermal/panel/full-by ticks,
PropertiesChanged snapshots of every object, which read the battery over
glink) and the LED-ring thread (charge colour re-read every 5 s, another
battery read). y700-whoawake measured 7 + 3 wakeups/s for the daemon with
the screen off: a 5 s round costs about 50 context switches because every
battery attribute is a glink round trip to the ADSP. The runtime CX-collapse
work (cx-collapse/RESEARCH.md 1.4) needs the system-wide wakeup rate under
50/s, so every periodic poller counts.

What: a new [poller] section in /etc/tb323fu/helper.toml
    poll_s = 5                 # as before while the display is on
    poll_s_display_off = 30    # while every connected DSI panel is DPMS Off; 0 = poll_s
read on every round by both the main loop and the LED thread, with the
panel state taken from /sys/class/drm/card*-DSI-*/{status,dpms} (connected
connectors only: the unplugged DP and the writeback connector report "On"
forever - the same mistake the y700 autobright once made). Defaults keep the
daemon's behaviour on screen; the owner can raise poll_s_display_off to 60.
Missing keys mean defaults (serde default), so an old helper.toml loads
unchanged, and the daemon writes the new section on its next save.

Side effects while the panel is off: PropertiesChanged for a charger plugged
in arrives up to poll_s_display_off later (the LED charge indicator too);
wifi_tick's once-a-minute re-check becomes once per 12 rounds; the kernel
auto-check cadence is time based and unaffected. With the display on nothing
changes. Not changed: tb323fu-ctl (no new sub-command; edit the file and
`tb323fu-ctl reload`), docs/helper-reference.md.

Build on the tablet (no cargo on the Win11 side; helper/README.md):
    sudo apt install cargo rustc            # Rust >= 1.80
    cd /usr/local/src/tb323fu-linux && git apply ~/helperd-slow-poll.patch
    cd helper && cargo build --release -p tb323fu-helperd
    install -m755 target/release/tb323fu-helperd /usr/local/libexec/tb323fu-helperd
    # the dpkg package tb323fu-helper 0.4.1 owns /usr/libexec/tb323fu-helperd and
    # /usr/lib/systemd/system/tb323fu-helperd.service: point the unit at our binary
    # with a drop-in instead of running install.sh (survives package upgrades;
    # the helper's self-update refuses dpkg-owned installs anyway)
    mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/tb323fu-helperd.service.d
    printf '[Service]
ExecStart=
ExecStart=/usr/local/libexec/tb323fu-helperd
'         > /etc/systemd/system/tb323fu-helperd.service.d/y700-local.conf
    systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart tb323fu-helperd
    tb323fu-ctl status      # still answers; grep poll_s /etc/tb323fu/helper.toml after the first save
Verify: y700-wakeups 60 --off -> tb323fu-helperd under "wakeups/s by woken
process" should show ~2 active seconds of 60 instead of 12.
Undo: rm the drop-in, systemctl daemon-reload, restart; the [poller] keys
are ignored by the stock daemon.

Rebuild after a tb323fu-helper package upgrade (y700-checklist warns when
the running binary is not ours or the package version changed).

